Exercise I: Userspace RCU &amp; Sorted Linked List (17 points)
RCU core primitives (rcu_read_lock, rcu_read_unlock, rcu_synchronize) are documented in 3. Read-Copy-Update (RCU).
Sorted RCU Linked List (print, delete, insert)
struct node {
  struct node* _Atomic next;
  int key;
  _Atomic int value;
};

pthread_mutex_t write_lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
struct node* _Atomic head = NULL;

void print(int key) {
  rcu_read_lock();
  struct node* cur = atomic_load(&amp;head);
  while (cur != NULL &amp;&amp; cur-&gt;key &lt; key)
    cur = atomic_load(&amp;cur-&gt;next);

  if (cur != NULL &amp;&amp; cur-&gt;key == key)
    printf(&quot;%d\n&quot;, atomic_load(&amp;cur-&gt;value));
  else
    printf(&quot;Not found\n&quot;);
  rcu_read_unlock();
}

void delete(int key) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&amp;write_lock);
  struct node* _Atomic* pred = &amp;head;
  struct node* cur = atomic_load(pred);

  while (cur != NULL &amp;&amp; cur-&gt;key &lt; key) {
    pred = &amp;cur-&gt;next;
    cur = atomic_load(pred);
  }

  struct node* deleted = NULL;
  if (cur != NULL &amp;&amp; cur-&gt;key == key) {
    deleted = cur;
    atomic_store(pred, atomic_load(&amp;cur-&gt;next)); // Phase 1: remove
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&amp;write_lock);

  if (deleted != NULL) {
    rcu_synchronize(); // Wait for all active readers
    free(deleted);     // Phase 2: safe free
  }
}

void insert(int key, int value) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&amp;write_lock);
  struct node* _Atomic* pred = &amp;head;
  struct node* cur = atomic_load(pred);

  while (cur != NULL &amp;&amp; cur-&gt;key &lt; key) {
    pred = &amp;cur-&gt;next;
    cur = atomic_load(pred);
  }

  if (cur != NULL &amp;&amp; cur-&gt;key == key) {
    atomic_store(&amp;cur-&gt;value, value); // update in place
  } else {
    struct node* n = malloc(sizeof(*n));
    n-&gt;key = key;
    atomic_store(&amp;n-&gt;value, value);
    atomic_store(&amp;n-&gt;next, cur);      // publish next BEFORE linking into list!
    atomic_store(pred, n);
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&amp;write_lock);
}

Exercise II: Course Questions (3 points)

Redo vs. Undo logs (1.5 pts): Used in Transactional Memory (and crash-consistent storage/databases) to guarantee atomicity of transactions/updates. A redo log (deferred update) buffers writes locally and applies them only on commit (fast aborts, avoids dirty reads/double-write conflicts). An undo log (immediate update) writes directly to memory while saving old values to roll back on abort (fast commits).
Memory ordering segfault (1.5 pts): msg = &quot;hello&quot;; ok = true; / while(!ok){} printf(msg); — see 4. Out-of-Order Execution &amp; Memory Ordering.

