Exercise I: Lock-Free Task-Stealing Scheduler &amp; Termination (17 points)
Data Structures (Q1–Q2)
Inspired by the Michael–Scott lock-free queue (with a dummy Fake node):
struct task_t {
  void (*func)(void*);
  void* arg;
  struct task_t* _Atomic next;
};

struct queue_t {
  struct task_t* _Atomic head;
  struct task_t* _Atomic tail;
};
enqueue(queue, task) and dequeue(queue) follow the exact Michael–Scott algorithm (remember: dequeue returns the payload from res-&gt;next and res-&gt;next becomes the new dummy node).
Lock-Free 3-Mode Termination Worker (Q6–Q7)
Global state:
size_t nb_threads;
struct queue_t* queues;
_Atomic size_t nb_stealers = 0;
_Atomic size_t nb_terminates = 0;
void* worker(void* arg) {
  struct queue_t* my_queue = (struct queue_t*)arg;
  struct task_t* task;

normal:
  task = dequeue(my_queue);
  if (task != NULL) {
    task-&gt;func(task-&gt;arg);
    goto normal;
  }
  atomic_fetch_add(&amp;nb_stealers, 1);

steal:
  for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; nb_threads; i++) {
    task = dequeue(&amp;queues[i]);
    if (task != NULL) {
      atomic_fetch_add(&amp;nb_stealers, -1);
      task-&gt;func(task-&gt;arg);
      goto normal;
    }
  }
  atomic_fetch_add(&amp;nb_terminates, 1);

termination:
  // If any thread is NOT in steal/termination mode (i.e. running a task that
  // could spawn new tasks), nb_stealers &lt; nb_threads -&gt; go back to steal mode!
  if (atomic_load(&amp;nb_stealers) &lt; nb_threads) {
    atomic_fetch_add(&amp;nb_terminates, -1);
    goto steal;
  }
  if (atomic_load(&amp;nb_terminates) &lt; nb_threads)
    goto termination;

  return NULL; // All threads have verified all queues empty and entered termination
}



                  Subtlety in Q7&#039;s Condition ( nb_stealers &lt; nb_threads vs != 0)



Notice that a thread entering termination mode keeps its increment in nb_stealers (it does not decrement nb_stealers when moving from steal to termination). Therefore, all threads are idle iff nb_stealers == nb_threads, and if any thread steals work and returns to normal mode, it decrements nb_stealers so nb_stealers &lt; nb_threads, waking terminating threads back into steal mode!



Exercise II: Course Questions (3 points)

HTM Redo Log &amp; Main Limitation (1 pt): A CPU’s L1 cache naturally acts as a redo log because speculative writes stay buffered in private cache lines until commit (discarded on abort) while cache coherence detects read/write conflicts automatically. Its main limitation is L1 cache capacity: transactions exceeding cache size (or hitting cache associativity evictions) always abort. See 4. Implementation Techniques: HTM, STM, HyTM.
Ticket Lock vs. Spinlock vs. MCS Lock (1 pt): A ticket lock is more efficient than a TAS spinlock because waiting threads spin on read-only atomic_loads instead of write-heavy atomic_exchanges, avoiding continuous cache-line invalidation storms (and ensuring FIFO fairness). An MCS lock improves on the ticket lock because each waiter spins on its own private cache line (my.is_free) rather than a single shared screen variable, avoiding broadcast invalidations on unlock. See 03 - Lock Algorithms and Futexes.
Interleaved vs. First-Touch under Unknown Behavior (1 pt): When application access patterns are unknown, interleaved evenly distributes pages across all NUMA nodes, balancing load across memory controllers and interconnects and avoiding severe single-node bottlenecks caused by first-touch if one thread initializes shared data. See 08 - NUMA Architecture (Extra).

Lab 02 — Lock Benchmarks and Futex Mutex
Up: Readme | Concept: 03 - Lock Algorithms and Futexes

Benchmark Architecture (locks.c)

CLI Arguments: nb-threads it csd cd algo (csd = critical section delay in ns, cd = compute delay outside CS in ns, algo \in none | posix | spinlock | ticket | mcs | futex).
Timing: clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &amp;ts) for monotonic nanosecond timing.
Start Barrier: All threads execute atomic_fetch_add(&amp;nb_started, 1); while (atomic_load(&amp;nb_started) &lt; n) asm_pause(); so contention begins simultaneously.
Integrity Check: Increment a non-atomic uint32_t z++ inside the CS and assert z == n * it at the end.
Reported Metric: Average lock acquire+release overhead = \frac{\sum \text{elapsed}_i \cdot 10^9}{n \cdot \text{it}} - \text{cd} - \text{csd}.

Custom Futex Syscall Wrapper (futexlock.c)
#define _GNU_SOURCE
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;
#include &lt;sys/syscall.h&gt;
#include &lt;linux/futex.h&gt;

long futex(void* addr1, int op, int val1, struct timespec* timeout,
           void* addr2, int val3) {
  return syscall(SYS_futex, addr1, op, val1, timeout, addr2, val3);
}

void futex_wait(void* addr, int val) {
  futex(addr, FUTEX_WAIT, val, NULL, NULL, 0);
}

void futex_wake(void* addr, int nb_threads) {
  futex(addr, FUTEX_WAKE, nb_threads, NULL, NULL, 0);
}

The 3-state futex mutex (FREE, BUSY_NO_WAITER, BUSY_WITH_WAITERS) is detailed in 4. Passive Waiting &amp; Linux Futexes.
Performance takeaway: Spinlock degrades sharply as thread count increases due to cache-line bouncing; Ticket and MCS scale better on high contention until #threads &gt; #cores, where MCS suffers convoy effects and passive Futex/POSIX locks win.

Lab 05 — NVMM Write-Ahead Cache (NVCache)
Up: Readme | Concept: 07 - Persistent Memory (NVMM and Crash Consistency)

Architecture (Dulong et al., DSN ‘21)
Accelerate durable pwrite + fsync by logging writes synchronously into a circular NVMM write-ahead log (my_pwrite), while an asynchronous cleanup thread drains committed entries to disk in batches.
Two-Cache-Line Log Entry (struct.h)
#define NB_ENTRIES (64 * 1024)
#define CACHE_LINE 64

struct operation {
  union {
    struct {
      _Atomic bool committed;
      int          fd;
      size_t       n;
      off_t        off;
    };
    char first_cache_line[CACHE_LINE];
  };
  union {
    char buf[CACHE_LINE];
    char second_cache_line[CACHE_LINE];
  };
};

struct nvmm {
  struct operation operation[NB_ENTRIES];
  size_t           tail; // persistent tail
};
Critical Persistence Ordering (soluce.c)

my_pwrite:

Claim slot index via CAS on _Atomic size_t head while (index + 1) % NB_ENTRIES != atomic_load(&amp;vtail).
Copy payload into cur-&gt;buf, pwb(cur-&gt;second_cache_line).
Fill fd, n, off, pwb(cur-&gt;first_cache_line), pfence() (data &amp; metadata durable before commit flag).
atomic_store(&amp;cur-&gt;committed, true), pwb(cur-&gt;first_cache_line), psync() (durable before returning).


cleanup_thread (Batching):

Drain up to NB_ENTRIES / 2 entries via pwrite + fsync (once per batch!).
Clear committed = false, flush with pwb + pfence.
Advance nvmm-&gt;tail = tail, pwb(&amp;nvmm-&gt;tail), psync().
Only then advance atomic_store(&amp;vtail, tail) — prevents the [[07 - Persistent Memory (NVMM and Crash Consistency)#5-circular-persistent-log—the-ptail-vs-vtail-bug|ptail/vtail wrap-around corruption bug]].


recovery(fd):

Scan circularly from nvmm-&gt;tail for NB_ENTRIES, replaying every op-&gt;committed entry to fd via pwrite, then fsync(fd).
Clear committed = false oldest-to-newest with pwb + pfence() after each (safe against crashes during recovery!).
Reset nvmm-&gt;tail = 0, pwb(&amp;nvmm-&gt;tail), psync().






                  Compilation Flag 



Always compile with gcc -march=native so __CLWB__ or __CLFLUSHOPT__ macros activate in nvmm.h.

Lab 06 — NUMA Latency Measurement (Extra)
Up: Readme | Concept: 08 - NUMA Architecture (Extra)

Experimental Setup (numa/main.c)

Arguments: test_pu test_node test_buffer_kb test_workload_kb test_config [noisy_node] where test_config \in none | spread | overload.
Topology extraction: Uses -lhwloc (HWLOC_OBJ_NUMANODE and HWLOC_OBJ_PU) to map every PU to its parent NUMA node.
Noisy threads: Run on all PUs except test_pu, continuously sweeping 32 MB buffers (&gt; L3) located on node (n + 1) % nb_nodes (spread) or noisy_node (overload).
Cache-line striding &amp; pointer chasing:
struct cache_line {
  union {
    char _content[64];
    int  value;
  };
};
Pre-populate lines[i].value as a single random Hamiltonian cycle visiting every cache line once, then time cur = lines[cur].value to measure true random access latency across L1 (2 KB), L2 (128 KB), L3 (1 MB), local DRAM (32 MB), remote DRAM, and contended (spread/overload) scenarios.

Parallel-Computing/LifeOfSNM/Life.c.content:
include “easypap.h”
#include “rle_lexer.h”
include &lt;omp.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;
#include &lt;sys/mman.h&gt;
#include &lt;unistd.h&gt;
define LIFE_COLOR (ezv_rgb (255, 255, 0))
typedef unsigned cell_t;
//
// “small cell”
typedef unsigned char scell_t;
static cell_t *_table = NULL, *_alternate_table = NULL;
// lazy change tables (indexed by tile)
// number of tiles
static size_t _ntx;
static size_t _nty;
static scell_t *_active_table = NULL;
static scell_t *_change_table = NULL;
static inline cell_t *table_cell (cell_t *restrict i, int y, int x)
{
return i + y * DIM + x;
}
static inline scell_t *table_scell (scell_t *restrict i, int ty, int tx)
{
return i + ty * _ntx + tx;
}
// This kernel does not directly work on cur_img/next_img.
// Instead, we use 2D arrays of boolean values, not colors
/#define cur_table(y, x) (*table_cell (_table, (y), (x)))
/#define next_table(y, x) (*table_cell (_alternate_table, (y), (x)))
// similar idea for lazy change-checking
/#define active_tile(ty, tx) (*table_scell (_active_table, ty, tx))
/#define changed_tile(ty, tx) (*table_scell (_change_table, ty, tx))
static inline void activate_neighbors (int ty, int tx)
{
for (int dy = -1; dy ⇐ 1; dy++)
for (int dx = -1; dx ⇐ 1; dx++) {
int ny = ty + dy;
int nx = tx + dx;
if (nx &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; nx &lt; _ntx &amp;&amp; ny &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; ny &lt; _nty)
active_tile (ny, nx) = true;
}
}
void lazy_init (void)
{
_ntx              = DIM / TILE_W;
_nty              = DIM / TILE_H;
const size_t size = _ntx * _nty * sizeof (scell_t);
_active_table = mmap (NULL, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
_change_table = mmap (NULL, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
for (size_t t = 0; t &lt; _ntx * _nty; ++t) {
_active_table [t] = 1;
}
}
void life_init (void)
{
// life_init may be (indirectly) called several times so we check if data were
// already allocated
if (_table == NULL) {
const unsigned size = DIM * DIM * sizeof (cell_t);
PRINT_DEBUG (&#039;u&#039;, &quot;Memory footprint = 2 x %d bytes\n&quot;, size);

_table = mmap (NULL, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
               MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);

_alternate_table = mmap (NULL, size, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
                         MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);

}
}
void life_finalize (void)
{
const unsigned size = DIM * DIM * sizeof (cell_t);
munmap (_table, size);
munmap (_alternate_table, size);
if (_active_table != NULL) {
munmap (_active_table, _ntx * _nty * sizeof (scell_t));
}
if (_change_table != NULL) {
munmap (_change_table, _ntx * _nty * sizeof (scell_t));
}
}
// This function is called whenever the graphical window needs to be refreshed
void life_refresh_img (void)
{
for (int i = 0; i &lt; DIM; i++)
for (int j = 0; j &lt; DIM; j++)
cur_img (i, j) = cur_table (i, j) * LIFE_COLOR;
}
static inline void swap_tables (void)
{
cell_t *tmp = _table;
_table           = _alternate_table;
_alternate_table = tmp;
}
///////////////////////////// Default tiling
int life_do_tile_default (int x, int y, int width, int height)
{
int change = 0;
for (int i = y; i &lt; y + height; i++)
for (int j = x; j &lt; x + width; j++)
if (j &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; j &lt; DIM - 1 &amp;&amp; i &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; i &lt; DIM - 1) {
    unsigned n  = 0;
    unsigned me = cur_table (i, j);

    for (int yloc = i - 1; yloc &lt; i + 2; yloc++)
      for (int xloc = j - 1; xloc &lt; j + 2; xloc++)
        if (xloc != j || yloc != i)
          n += cur_table (yloc, xloc);

    if (me == 1 &amp;&amp; n != 2 &amp;&amp; n != 3) {
      me     = 0;
      change = 1;
    } else if (me == 0 &amp;&amp; n == 3) {
      me     = 1;
      change = 1;
    }

    next_table (i, j) = me;
  }

return change;
}
///////////////////////////// Sequential version (seq)
//
unsigned life_compute_seq (unsigned nb_iter)
{
for (unsigned it = 1; it ⇐ nb_iter; it++) {
int change = do_tile (0, 0, DIM, DIM);

if (!change)
  return it;

swap_tables ();

}
return 0;
}
///////////////////////////// Tiled sequential version (tiled)
//
unsigned life_compute_tiled (unsigned nb_iter)
{
unsigned res = 0;
for (unsigned it = 1; it ⇐ nb_iter; it++) {
unsigned change = 0;
for (int y = 0; y &lt; DIM; y += TILE_H)
  for (int x = 0; x &lt; DIM; x += TILE_W)
    change |= do_tile (x, y, TILE_W, TILE_H);

swap_tables ();

if (!change) { // we stop if all cells are stable
  res = it;
  break;
}

}
return res;
}
/**
*

openMP version of the tiled implementation


*/
unsigned life_compute_openmp (unsigned nb_iter)
{
unsigned res = 0;
for (unsigned it = 1; it ⇐ nb_iter; it++) {
unsigned change = 0;
/#pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(| : change)
for (int y = 0; y &lt; DIM; y += TILE_H)
for (int x = 0; x &lt; DIM; x += TILE_W)
change |= do_tile (x, y, TILE_W, TILE_H);
swap_tables ();

if (!change) { // we stop if all cells are stable
  res = it;
  break;
}

}
return res;
}
/**
*

lazy openMP version of the tiled implementation


*/
unsigned life_compute_lazy (unsigned nb_iter)
{
if (_active_table == NULL) {
lazy_init ();
}
unsigned res = 0;
for (unsigned it = 1; it ⇐ nb_iter; it++) {
unsigned change = 0;
/#pragma omp parallel
{
/#pragma omp for collapse(2) reduction(| : change)                              
schedule(guided, 8 * TILE_H * TILE_W)
for (int ty = 0; ty &lt; _nty; ++ty)
for (int tx = 0; tx &lt; _ntx; ++tx) {
// reset changed
changed_tile (ty, tx) = 0;
// only compute do_tile on possibly changed tiles
if (active_tile (ty, tx)) {
changed_tile (ty, tx) =
do_tile (tx * TILE_W, ty * TILE_H, TILE_W, TILE_H);
        change |= changed_tile (ty, tx);
        // deactivate it before computing next timestep active_tile state
        active_tile (ty, tx) = 0;
      }
    }
}
swap_tables ();

if (!change) { // we stop if all cells are stable
  res = it;
  break;
}
// update active_tile sequentially ( 9 * (number of changed tiles) writes)
for (int ty = 0; ty &lt; _nty; ++ty) {
  for (int tx = 0; tx &lt; _ntx; ++tx) {
    if (changed_tile (ty, tx)) {
      activate_neighbors (ty, tx);
    }
  }
}

}
return res;
}
